STRFTIME(3)                    P32W Unix                     STRFTIME(3)

NAME
       strftime, clock, difftime - format times, CPU time, differences

SYNOPSIS
       #include <time.h>

       size_t strftime(char *s, size_t max, const char *fmt,
                       const struct tm *tm);
       clock_t clock(void);
       double difftime(time_t t1, time_t t0);

DESCRIPTION
       strftime writes the time tm (from localtime or gmtime, see
       time(3)) into s as fmt says, at most max bytes with the
       final '\0'. Characters of fmt are copied, except these
       conversions (the "C" locale's):

       %a %A  weekday: Wed, Wednesday
       %b %B  month: Oct, October (%h is %b)
       %c     date and time: %a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y
       %C     century, 2 digits
       %d %e  day of the month, 01-31 and (space-padded) 1-31
       %D %x  %m/%d/%y
       %F     %Y-%m-%d
       %H %I  hour, 00-23 and 01-12
       %j     day of the year, 001-366
       %m %M  month 01-12; minute 00-59
       %n %t  a newline; a tab
       %p     AM or PM
       %r %R  %I:%M:%S %p; %H:%M
       %S     second, 00-60
       %T %X  %H:%M:%S
       %u %w  weekday as a number: 1-7 from Monday; 0-6 from Sunday
       %U %W  week of the year, 00-53, from the first Sunday
              (%U) or Monday (%W)
       %V %G %g
              ISO 8601 week (01-53: weeks start on Monday and week
              1 holds the year's first Thursday), its year, and
              that year's last 2 digits
       %y %Y  year: 2 digits; all of it
       %z %Z  the time zone: +hhmm from UTC; its name (TZ, see
              tzset(3))
       %%     a %

       %E and %O before a conversion are accepted and ignored.

       clock returns the CPU time the process has used, user and
       system, in ticks: CLOCKS_PER_SEC (50) a second.

       difftime returns t1 - t0 in seconds.

RETURN VALUE
       strftime returns the number of characters written, not
       counting the '\0', or 0 if they did not fit in max bytes
       (s is then undefined).

EXAMPLES
       char buf[64];
       time_t t = time(0);
       strftime(buf, sizeof buf, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M", localtime(&t));

       clock_t c = clock();
       ...
       printf("%.2f s\n", (double)(clock() - c) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC);

SEE ALSO
       time(3), mktime(3), tzset(3), date(1), times(2)
